          "id": "AALA20260114",
          "title": "Automated feedback in language education and beyond: GenAI opportunities and challenges",
          "abstract": "Research suggests that feedback processes are challenging for teachers to manage. To what extent does automated feedback, particularly from GenAI, offer part of a solution or does it instead present new and different challenges? In this paper, I discuss potential strengths and limitations of automated feedback sources, and indicate what distinctive attributes that humans have to offer. I draw on two ongoing research projects related to student use of GenAI in university language education in China. The first compared feedback enactment amongst students using either Automated Writing Evaluation or GenAI for revising English academic writing, theoretically framed through feedback literacy concepts (Yang, Carless and Lin, ongoing). Data sources were a feedback literacy questionnaire completed at two time-points, interviews with students, and analysis of their written drafts and revisions. The second study investigated the evolution of EAP university students’ metaphorical conceptualizations of GenAI through the theoretical lens of Actor-Network Theory. Data collection focused on interviews with eleven carefully selected students at two points over one year, to capture their changing perceptions of GenAI’s role in academic writing (Zhou & Carless, ongoing). A key finding from the first study was that the enactment of automated feedback involved different patterns of surface and deep cognitive engagement, mediated by student feedback literacy. A striking finding from the second study was the personal and affective connection that some students seemed to form with GenAI, including metaphors of collaborative mentor, teacher, friend and even butler. I conclude by demonstrating significance through a framework summarizing potentials and limitations of automated feedback from GenAI. Strengths of GenAI include its speed, detail and supportive tone in offering emotionally safe feedback. Its limitations include hallucinations, ethical concerns, and the risk of student over-reliance. Key potential human contributions include modelling critical evaluative judgment and demonstrating empathy.",
