          "id": "AALA2026059",
          "title": "What critical thinking skills are measured by each item of the English Critical Thinking Test (ECTT)?",
          "abstract": "This study investigates the assessment design of higher-order critical thinking (CT) test items in English language assessment. Traditional language tests often assume that each item measures a single latent ability or skill. However, higher-order cognitive processes such as critical thinking typically require the integration of multiple subskills to solve a question. This raises the question of whether such items can be adequately represented by single-skill measurement models. The purpose of this study was to examine which CT skills are measured by individual items of the English Critical Thinking Test (ECTT) and to explore the multidimensional structure of higher-order CT assessment. The ECTT was designed based on seven CT subskills assumed to underlie higher-order reasoning in English reading tasks. Participants were 350 Japanese university EFL students (A1–B2 proficiency levels). The ECTT consisted of 41 multiple-choice items. Prior to the analysis, three experts independently constructed Q-matrices by assigning one or two intended CT skills from seven predefined skills to each item. Students then took the ECTT. To examine the skill structure of the items, cognitive diagnostic models (CDMs) were applied using the R statistical software. The results were compared with findings from factor analysis and structural equation modeling (SEM) to evaluate the dimensionality of the test. The CDM results indicated that models assuming multiple CT skills per item provided a good fit to the data. Factor analysis also revealed substantial cross-loadings across factors, and models that allowed cross-loadings demonstrated improved model fit. However, the results were difficult to interpret because items intended to measure the same CT skills loaded on different combinations of factors. These findings highlight the importance of data-informed approaches to assessment design and suggest that multidimensional measurement frameworks may be necessary when assessing higher-order cognitive skills in language testing.",
