          "id": "AALA2026014",
          "title": "‘Traditional’ and ‘efficient’ writing assessment tasks: Do they support similar interpretations of writing ability?",
          "abstract": "In response to increasing demands for practicality and improved test taker experience, large-scale language assessments have begun to adopt shorter, more efficient writing tasks (Naismith et al., 2024; Sasayama et al., 2021). Establishing the validity of score interpretations from these tasks has become a critical concern (Kane, 2021). In particular, questions remain regarding the degree to which efficient and traditional writing tasks support similar interpretations about writing ability. To address this concern, the present study examined both written products and writing processes elicited by two writing tasks in a standardized English language assessment context. Data were collected from 1,059 Chinese L2 English learners who completed a traditional independent argumentative essay (30 minutes) and a shorter, efficient academic discussion task (10 minutes). Written responses were scored by trained raters using official scoring rubrics. Textual features related to length, linguistic complexity and accuracy, and text overlap with task input were extracted using natural language processing tools. To examine writing processes, keystroke-logging data were analyzed to derive features related to planning, pausing, and revision behaviors. Linear mixed-effects models were used to examine task effects, score effects, and their interactions across product and process features. Results showed substantial comparability between the two tasks. Holistic task scores were highly similar across task types and most textual and keystroke features showed either no task differences or only small effects. These findings suggest that the efficient task could elicit broadly similar linguistic and process features of academic writing ability. Importantly, both product and process features differentiated score levels in largely similar ways across tasks. The results provide empirical support for the claim that both tasks support similar interpretations regarding writing ability and suggest that efficient tasks can serve as a viable option in large-scale language assessment. Implications for assessment design and validation are discussed.",
