          "id": "AALA2026007",
          "title": "Leveraging large language models for automatic assessment of English-Chinese interpreting: Optimizing in-context learning examples for few-shot prompting",
          "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used as scoring agents for productive language performance, including translation and interpreting (T&I). In LLM-based scoring, few-shot prompting, which provides scored exemplars to guide LLMs, can potentially enhance scoring accuracy, yet the design of exemplars remains under-explored in language assessment research. Grounded in a measurement perspective that treats prompting and exemplar policies as part of the scoring instrument, this study investigates how exemplar design and optimization shapes automated scoring outcomes.\nUsing the Interpreting Quality Evaluation Corpus (IQEC; > 1,700 human-scored English-Chinese renditions across multiple interpreting modes), we conducted three inter-linked studies that systematically manipulated five exemplar-design factors: exemplar quantity (shot size), exemplar label/score quality, exemplar-test similarity, label/score distribution (sampling strategy), and exemplar ordering within prompts. LLM scoring performance was evaluated across classification- and prediction-oriented metrics (e.g., agreement, correlation, and error indices) against human benchmarks.\nResults converged on four key patterns. First, increasing shot size generally improved scoring from 1 to 8 exemplars, but gains often diminished and even reversed beyond 8. Second, high-quality labels produced more accurate scoring, especially when exemplars shared the same source speeches as the test set, highlighting the importance of exemplar-test similarity. Third, stratified sampling that preserved the original score distribution consistently improved performance. Fourth, ordering exemplars from low to high performance bands tended to yield additional gains.\nThese findings provide evidence-based guidance for designing few-shot prompts in LLM-based (interpreting) assessment and suggest that operational deployment should adopt transparent, auditable exemplar-selection policies to support more reliable and valid automated scoring in large-scale contexts.",
