          "id": "AALA2026043",
          "title": "Criterion difficulty in IELTS Academic Writing Task 1: Evidence from line chart description tasks",
          "abstract": "Writing has long been a highly challenging skill for a number of IELTS test-takers, so teaching and learning how to write effectively has been an important goal for both IELTS instructors and students. As one of the two components of IELTS writing, IELTS writing task 1 often requires its test takers to summarize and describe visual information using clear and accurate language. While the IELTS analytic rubric evaluating performance across four criteria (Task Achievement, Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy) is widely used, limited research has explored whether these criteria pose different levels of difficulty for EFL learners in Task 1 writing, thus guiding adjustments to the teaching and learning practices. This study aims to investigate (1) which IELTS Writing Task 1 scoring criteria are most challenging for EFL students as they describe line graphs, (2) whether students perform differently when coping with single-chart or multiple-chart tasks, and (3) whether criterion difficulty varies across the two line-chart task types. A total of 85 Vietnamese EFL high school students enrolled in an IELTS preparation course produced two Task 1 responses under exam-like conditions: one describing a single-chart task and the other a multiple-chart task. The responses were evaluated using the official IELTS band descriptors. Descriptive statistics and a two-way repeated measures ANOVA were then conducted to analyze the test scores. The findings are expected to provide insights into criterion difficulty and task-related challenges in IELTS Task 1 writing, offering pedagogical implications for targeted instruction in IELTS preparation courses.",
