          "id": "AALA2026009",
          "title": "Beyond Agreement: Modeling the Measurement Behavior of Generative AI Scoring Configurations in Integrated Writing Assessment",
          "abstract": "The increasing use of generative AI in writing assessment has prompted growing interest in its potential to support scalable evaluation. However, most existing research has focused primarily on agreement between AI and human scores (Jia & Araydoust, 2024; Kim et al., 2024; Manning et al., 2025), offering limited insight into how AI scoring systems function as measurement processes. Responding to calls for more human-centered and transparent approaches to assessment innovation, this study moves beyond agreement statistics to examine the measurement behavior of generative AI in integrated writing assessment. Two research questions guided the study: (1) Do AI scoring configurations differ systematically in severity and bias within a measurement framework? and (2) Which configurations demonstrate the strongest alignment with human final scores?\nUsing a fully crossed design, 314 L2 English integrated writing responses were scored under 12 AI configurations varying by platform (ChatGPT 4.1 and Gemini 2.5 Pro), prompting strategy (zero-shot and few-shot), and temperature setting (0.0, 0.2, & 0.5). Many-facet Rasch measurement (MFRM) modeled essays as the object of measurement and AI configurations as rater-like facets to estimate severity and bias. Results revealed substantial platform-related severity differences, with Gemini 2.5 Pro producing systematically more severe scores than ChatGPT 4.1, whereas prompting and temperature effects were comparatively small. Bias analyses indicated no substantively meaningful interaction effects, and fit statistics for rater-like facets fell within acceptable ranges. Complementary analyses using linear mixed-effects modeling and quadratic weighted kappa showed stronger alignment between Gemini configurations and human final scores. Across conditions, Gemini 2.5 Pro consistently demonstrated higher agreement with human ratings than ChatGPT 4.1, with the highest agreement observed under few-shot prompting at temperature 0.0. Overall, platform choice emerged as the primary source of systematic variation in AI-generated holistic scores, contributing validity evidence for more transparent and accountable AI integration in integrated writing assessment.",
