          "id": "AALA2026079",
          "title": "Harmonious Discourse Analysis of the Reading Discourses in NMET from the Perspective of Transitivity: Taking the 2025 NMET as an Example",
          "abstract": "Against the backdrop of advancing ecological civilization and the fundamental task of moral education, ecolinguistics provides a brand-new theoretical perspective for interpreting the value of educational discourse. The English reading discourses of National Matriculation English Test (NMET) serve as the core carrier for language learning, assessment and value guidance in the basic education stage, and their inherent harmonious value and ecological orientation exert a positive guiding effect on foreign language teaching. As the core framework of localized ecological discourse analysis in China, Harmonious Discourse Analysis (HDA) has laid a theoretical foundation for research on the ecological value of educational discourse. Taking the reading discourses from the 2025 NMET as the research object, this study constructs an analytical framework based on the transitivity system of Systemic Functional Linguistics (SFL), the HDA of ecolinguistics, and the three themes proposed in the General Senior High School English Curriculum Standards. The study first conducts corpus annotation and quantitative statistics, and then carries out qualitative analysis in conjunction with the general \"human-centered\" assumption and three principles of HDA. The results show that the distribution of transitivity resources in different themes presents significant differentiated characteristics highly compatible with the thematic connotations. Through the strategic selection of the transitivity system, the texts construct differentiated harmonious discourse systems in the three themes, fully implementing the core criteria of HDA, and ultimately convey a trinity of harmonious value concepts: the self-view of harmonious physical and mental development, the social view of service, dedication and symbiosis, and the ecological view of the community of human and nature. This study aims to enrich localized ecolinguistics research on NMET discourses, and provide theoretical references and practical implications for the design of human-centered assessment, , its alignment with Curriculum Standards, and the teaching of ecological literacy in senior high school English reading.",
