          "id": "AALA2026047",
          "title": "Assessing Advanced Chinese Reading Literacy: Designing Print and Digital Tasks for Secondary Students in Singapore",
          "abstract": "The assessment of reading literacy remains an important challenge in multilingual education systems, particularly when learners are expected to develop high-level reading competencies across both print and digital environments. In Singapore, Secondary Higher Chinese Language (HCL) students are positioned as near-native bilingual learners and are expected to demonstrate advanced Chinese reading literacy, including interpretive, inferential, evaluative, and integrative competencies. Yet relatively little empirical research has examined how such competencies can be systematically assessed or how students perform across different reading modalities.\nThis paper presents the design of a large-scale mixed-methods study investigating the reading literacy, motivation, and reading practices of Secondary HCL students in Singapore, with particular attention to how assessment tasks can be used to examine students’ cognitive reading subskills. Grounded in a pragmatic research paradigm, the study integrates document analysis, assessment development, questionnaire data, and qualitative interviews to provide a comprehensive account of advanced Chinese reading literacy.\nApproximately 300 Secondary Three HCL students from six to eight secondary schools, including both Special Assistance Plan (SAP) and non-SAP schools, will participate in the study. Two complementary assessment components have been developed. The first is a syllabus-aligned print-based Chinese reading assessment designed to measure students’ performance in key cognitive reading subskills. The second is a digital Chinese reading assessment administered via an online platform and informed by the Programme for International Student Assessment (PISA) reading literacy framework, modelling contemporary multimodal and multi-source reading environments. Assessment items will be classified by subject matter experts according to established cognitive reading subskills, enabling the study to analyse students’ performance across different reading processes and task types.\nThe conference presentation will discuss the conceptualisation of the study, principles guiding assessment design, and preliminary findings from the pilot phase, including insights into task design, patterns in students’ responses, and the alignment between assessment tasks and targeted reading subskills.",
