          "id": "AALA20260153",
          "title": "Human Values in AI-Mediated Writing: EFL Learners’ Beliefs and Assessment Tensions",
          "abstract": "Despite the growing prevalence of AI-mediated writing in university contexts, academic essay assessment remains anchored in standardized criteria such as explicit thesis statements, logical development, coherence, and rhetorical control. Yet little is known about how learners’ beliefs about valued writing qualities shape their engagement with these criteria and their stance toward AI use. This gap foregrounds a key question for humanistic assessment: how can writing assessment account for learner values while maintaining fairness and accountability?\nThis classroom-based study investigates how Chinese EFL learners negotiate valued writing qualities as they engage with academic essay assessment and AI-mediated writing practices. Data are drawn from 120 first-year university students and include: (1) Week 1 discussion posts on “what makes good writing,” (2) a personal writing task with AI-use disclosures, (3) a timed academic essay, (4) a questionnaire measuring learners’ understanding of key academic writing constructs and orientations toward AI-assisted writing, and (5) ongoing longitudinal revisions of the timed essay across instructional phases, with permitted AI use and required disclosure (data collection in progress).\nThe analysis addresses three questions: (1) what qualities learners associate with good writing and academic writing; (2) how these beliefs relate to writing performance; and (3) how they interact with learners’ AI use. Preliminary findings indicate a persistent tension: while students recognize academic writing expectations, they continue to value sincerity, authenticity, and personal voice. These values are reflected in their cautious or selective use of AI.\nThe study examines the relationships among learner beliefs, writing performance, and AI-use stances across tasks. It argues that in AI-mediated contexts, writing assessment should extend beyond a narrow focus on textual features to more explicitly account for learner authorship, meaning-making, and the translation of human values into academic writing. Such an approach contributes to more valid, transparent, and humanistic assessment practices.",
