          "id": "AALA20260115",
          "title": "Student Agency and Autonomous AI Use in EFL Writing Peer Assessment: A Self-Determination Theory Perspective",
          "abstract": "Existing AI-integrated peer assessment in EFL writing is predominantly teacher-led, with instructors dictating how students should use AI tools. This not only restricts student agency and deviates from the humanistic pursuit of learner subjectivity, but also underutilizes AI’s potential for personalized learning. To activate student agency, this study adopted a student-led autonomous AI use model, in which students were granted full autonomy to decide whether, when, and how to employ AI tools in EFL writing peer assessment. Guided by Self-Determination Theory (SDT), which highlights autonomy, competence, and relatedness as core motivators for meaningful learning, this study aims to explore students’ distinct AI use patterns, underlying psychological and contextual factors, and students’ attitudes toward the model. Data were collected from reflective reports, group presentation slides, and semi-structured interviews and analyzed using Thematic Analysis (TA). Preliminary findings reveal diverse AI application patterns shaped by the interaction of contextual conditions and students’ psychological needs, as well as multifaceted perceptions of student-led AI use in peer assessment. It is hoped that this study could highlight the significance of integrating student agency and autonomy in the AI era and provide empirical references for constructing humanistic and student-centered language assessment.",
