          "id": "AALA20260196",
          "title": "ESP or EMI? Content or Language or Both?",
          "abstract": "The current paper reports on the results of an experiment in assessment of two courses, English for Tourism and Business Communication, in the program Bachelor of English Language at the University of Languages and International Studies, Vietnam National University, Hanoi. The courses are for students at final years to identify their graduation stream (as Tourism or Business) within the English major. The objectives of these courses are to improve English proficiency related to the specific profession (thus ESP?) and to equip students with professional knowledge of tourism or business communication. The courses are taught entirely in English (so EMI?). The paper first discusses the blurry distinction between ESP and EMI in such cases, and then poses the issues such ambiguity causes to assessment. It has been a long and unconclusive discussion among ESP and EMI educators and assessors of which constructs should be assessed: content knowledge, or English proficiency, or both of them, in which case what the weighting should be. In the current experiment, students’ assignments are assessed by three different groups of raters: experts in the professional field (tour managers or business managers), English lecturers, and the lecturers of the courses. The first group assess the quality of the content related to their professional field only; the second group of raters focus on the language quality only; while the third use the course rubric assessing both the English language and the professional content with a 50%-50% weighting. The scores will be statistically analyzed using ANOVA and correlations to explore the divergence or convergence in assessment of content versus language versus both. The findings are expected to yield useful implications to educators of ESP or EMI programs at tertiary education.",
