          "id": "AALA20260193",
          "title": "Modeling the Interaction between Critical AI Literacy, Writing Self-Efficacy, and GenAI Dependence in EAP Writing",
          "abstract": "The widespread integration of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) in academic settings has precipitated concerns regarding student autonomy and AI dependence. While Critical AI Literacy (CAL) has been proposed to mitigate this over-reliance, its operational mechanisms within the English for Academic Purpose (EAP) context remain underexplored. EAP is essential for English major university students during the essay drafting and revision as it requires high English proficiency; thereby, the reliance of AI is increasingly likely to occur while students completing unfamiliar EAP task. However, there is a paucity of empirical evidence regarding how a student’s EAP writing self-efficacy (WSE) interacts with their critical AI literacy to influence subsequent AI dependency. Hence, this proposed study aims to investigate the interplay between CAL and WSE, and their collective impact on the AI dependence of Chinese English major university students. The primary objective is to determine the extent to which WSE mediates the relationship between CAL and reliance on GenAI during drafting and revision processes. Adopting a cross-sectional study in China where language barriers make EAP more challenged, the study will recruit a sample of 200 to 300 Chinese coastal region university students who are majored in English. Concurrently, participants’ CAL, WSE and AI dependence levels will be measured using adapted, validated psychometric questionnaires. The resulting data will be subjected to Structural Equation Modelling (SEM) to evaluate the interaction effects. It is anticipated that the findings will demonstrate WSE as a critical mediating variable, suggesting that high CAL only reduces AI dependence when coupled with high WSE. Consequently, this research will offer an original contribution to knowledge by re-conceptualizing CAL theory and informing pedagogical strategies designed to foster learner autonomy in an increasingly AI-mediated educational landscape.",
